1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,535
Welcome to episode ten.

2
00:00:01,575 --> 00:00:04,275
Now that we can calibrate a calorimeter,

3
00:00:04,315 --> 00:00:09,138
we can use it to measure the enthalpy
change of a reaction in solution.

4
00:00:09,178 --> 00:00:12,631
Today: neutralisation
reactions, limiting reagents,

5
00:00:12,671 --> 00:00:15,617
and choosing exactly
the right denominator.

6
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:17,297
Two quick checks.

7
00:00:17,337 --> 00:00:23,748
What amount of sodium hydroxide is in fifty
point zero millilitres of a zero point

8
00:00:23,788 --> 00:00:26,895
nine zero zero moles per litre solution?

9
00:00:26,935 --> 00:00:33,188
And a calorimeter with a calibration factor
of five hundred and forty joules per

10
00:00:33,228 --> 00:00:36,650
degree warms by two
point zero zero degrees.

11
00:00:36,690 --> 00:00:38,695
How much heat did it gain?

12
00:00:38,735 --> 00:00:40,072
Pause and answer.

13
00:00:48,533 --> 00:00:54,695
Convert to litres first: zero point zero
five zero zero litres times zero point

14
00:00:54,735 --> 00:00:59,248
nine zero zero gives zero point
zero four five zero moles.

15
00:00:59,288 --> 00:01:05,449
And five hundred and forty times two
point zero zero is one thousand and eighty

16
00:01:05,489 --> 00:01:06,289
joules.

17
00:01:06,999 --> 00:01:08,858
Here's a typical experiment.

18
00:01:08,898 --> 00:01:12,723
Seventy-five point zero
millilitres of hydrochloric acid,

19
00:01:12,763 --> 00:01:15,842
at zero point eight zero
zero moles per litre,

20
00:01:15,882 --> 00:01:19,435
and fifty point zero
millilitres of sodium hydroxide,

21
00:01:19,475 --> 00:01:24,927
at zero point nine zero zero moles per
litre, both start at the same temperature.

22
00:01:24,967 --> 00:01:27,815
They're mixed in a calibrated calorimeter.

23
00:01:28,199 --> 00:01:33,730
The reaction is hydrochloric acid plus
sodium hydroxide forming sodium chloride and

24
00:01:33,770 --> 00:01:34,133
water.

25
00:01:34,173 --> 00:01:37,663
Every reactant and product
has a coefficient of one.

26
00:01:37,999 --> 00:01:41,814
Available amounts, with
volumes converted to litres.

27
00:01:41,854 --> 00:01:47,744
Hydrochloric acid: zero point zero seven
five zero litres times zero point eight

28
00:01:47,784 --> 00:01:51,376
zero zero is zero point
zero six zero zero moles.

29
00:01:51,416 --> 00:01:57,455
Sodium hydroxide: zero point zero five zero
zero litres times zero point nine zero

30
00:01:57,495 --> 00:02:00,830
zero is zero point zero
four five zero moles.

31
00:02:01,199 --> 00:02:03,021
Notice what we didn't do.

32
00:02:03,061 --> 00:02:08,530
We didn't add the volumes or the
concentrations together to get an amount.

33
00:02:08,570 --> 00:02:14,153
Each solution's amount comes from its
own volume and its own concentration.

34
00:02:15,132 --> 00:02:19,881
With a one to one ratio, the
smaller amount limits the reaction.

35
00:02:19,921 --> 00:02:25,009
Sodium hydroxide, zero point zero
four five zero moles, is limiting.

36
00:02:25,399 --> 00:02:30,531
So zero point zero four five zero
moles of each reactant is used,

37
00:02:30,571 --> 00:02:34,987
and zero point zero four five
zero moles of water forms.

38
00:02:35,027 --> 00:02:41,591
Hydrochloric acid is in excess: zero point
zero six zero zero minus zero point zero

39
00:02:41,631 --> 00:02:47,042
four five zero leaves zero point
zero one five zero moles unreacted.

40
00:02:47,399 --> 00:02:52,571
The heat released comes only from
the reaction that actually happens,

41
00:02:52,611 --> 00:02:55,291
so it's set by the limiting reagent.

42
00:02:55,331 --> 00:03:00,503
The excess acid just sits in the
solution and releases no extra heat,

43
00:03:00,543 --> 00:03:01,828
under this model.

44
00:03:02,798 --> 00:03:05,686
The calorimeter measures
the heat it gained:

45
00:03:05,726 --> 00:03:10,943
q for the calorimeter equals the calibration
factor times the temperature rise.

46
00:03:10,983 --> 00:03:14,510
For an exothermic reaction,
that's a positive number.

47
00:03:14,865 --> 00:03:17,957
That heat came from the reacting system.

48
00:03:17,997 --> 00:03:21,950
So, assuming all the heat
stays in the calorimeter,

49
00:03:21,990 --> 00:03:26,649
q for the reaction is the negative
of q for the calorimeter.

50
00:03:26,689 --> 00:03:28,646
Same size, opposite sign.

51
00:03:28,998 --> 00:03:34,842
To get a molar enthalpy, divide by the
amount that the enthalpy refers to.

52
00:03:34,882 --> 00:03:41,282
If we want delta H per mole of water
formed, divide by the moles of water formed.

53
00:03:41,322 --> 00:03:44,940
That's the amount set by
the limiting reagent,

54
00:03:44,980 --> 00:03:50,228
not the amount of whichever reagent
happens to be mentioned first.

55
00:03:51,198 --> 00:03:53,412
What if the temperature falls?

56
00:03:53,452 --> 00:03:59,047
Suppose zero point zero five zero zero
moles of a salt, which we'll call Y,

57
00:03:59,087 --> 00:04:04,982
dissolves in a calorimeter with a
calibration factor of five hundred joules per

58
00:04:05,022 --> 00:04:11,143
degree, and the temperature drops from
twenty-one point zero to eighteen point six

59
00:04:11,183 --> 00:04:11,744
degrees.

60
00:04:11,784 --> 00:04:13,773
What's the sign of delta H?

61
00:04:13,813 --> 00:04:15,090
Pause and decide.

62
00:04:23,531 --> 00:04:26,720
The temperature change
is final minus initial:

63
00:04:26,760 --> 00:04:30,019
eighteen point six minus
twenty-one point zero,

64
00:04:30,059 --> 00:04:33,248
which is negative two
point four zero degrees.

65
00:04:33,288 --> 00:04:38,582
So q for the calorimeter is five hundred
times negative two point four zero:

66
00:04:38,622 --> 00:04:41,460
negative one thousand two hundred joules.

67
00:04:41,500 --> 00:04:43,466
The calorimeter lost energy.

68
00:04:43,864 --> 00:04:47,267
That energy went into
the dissolving process,

69
00:04:47,307 --> 00:04:52,239
so q for the process is positive
one thousand two hundred joules.

70
00:04:52,279 --> 00:04:55,911
Divided by zero point zero
five zero zero moles,

71
00:04:55,951 --> 00:05:01,113
delta H is positive twenty-four
point zero kilojoules per mole of Y.

72
00:05:01,153 --> 00:05:06,545
A temperature fall means an endothermic
process and a positive delta H.

73
00:05:06,585 --> 00:05:11,481
Keep the sign of delta T, and the
signs take care of themselves.

74
00:05:12,464 --> 00:05:18,241
Practice question Q nineteen uses exactly
the amounts we've just worked out.

75
00:05:18,281 --> 00:05:20,001
It's worth eight marks.

76
00:05:20,041 --> 00:05:22,108
Pause the video and try it.

77
00:05:37,531 --> 00:05:42,126
Parts a and b: zero point zero
six zero zero moles of acid,

78
00:05:42,166 --> 00:05:46,604
zero point zero four five zero
moles of sodium hydroxide,

79
00:05:46,644 --> 00:05:52,811
so sodium hydroxide is limiting, leaving
zero point zero one five zero moles of

80
00:05:52,851 --> 00:05:54,029
acid unreacted.

81
00:05:54,398 --> 00:05:54,861
Part c.

82
00:05:54,901 --> 00:06:00,614
The temperature rose from twenty point
zero zero to twenty-four point three zero

83
00:06:00,654 --> 00:06:04,138
degrees: a rise of four
point three zero degrees.

84
00:06:04,178 --> 00:06:08,668
The calibration factor was measured
for these exact conditions,

85
00:06:08,708 --> 00:06:14,421
so q for the calorimeter is five hundred
and ninety times four point three zero:

86
00:06:14,461 --> 00:06:18,056
two thousand five hundred
and thirty-seven joules.

87
00:06:18,398 --> 00:06:20,115
Before part d, predict.

88
00:06:20,155 --> 00:06:25,996
Neutralising a strong acid with a strong
base releases roughly fifty to sixty

89
00:06:26,036 --> 00:06:28,212
kilojoules per mole of water.

90
00:06:28,252 --> 00:06:31,765
So expect a negative
value of about that size.

91
00:06:32,131 --> 00:06:32,610
Part d.

92
00:06:32,650 --> 00:06:36,831
The water formed is zero point
zero four five zero moles.

93
00:06:36,871 --> 00:06:41,646
So delta H equals negative two
point five three seven kilojoules,

94
00:06:41,686 --> 00:06:45,201
divided by zero point zero
four five zero moles:

95
00:06:45,241 --> 00:06:50,090
negative fifty-six point four
kilojoules per mole of water formed.

96
00:06:50,130 --> 00:06:56,203
It's negative because the reaction released
heat, and it's in the predicted range.

97
00:06:56,598 --> 00:07:01,833
Marks: two for the amounts, two for
the limiting reagent and excess,

98
00:07:01,873 --> 00:07:07,302
two for the heat, and two for the
correct denominator, sign and value.

99
00:07:07,664 --> 00:07:10,453
Three tempting denominators, all wrong.

100
00:07:10,493 --> 00:07:15,314
Dividing by the acid supplied, zero
point zero six zero zero moles,

101
00:07:15,354 --> 00:07:17,998
gives negative forty-two point three.

102
00:07:18,038 --> 00:07:23,366
Dividing by the total moles of both
reactants gives an even smaller value.

103
00:07:23,406 --> 00:07:28,734
And dividing by the total volume gives
a quantity in kilojoules per litre,

104
00:07:28,774 --> 00:07:31,345
which isn't a molar enthalpy at all.

105
00:07:31,385 --> 00:07:37,116
The first wrong step in each is not asking
how much reaction actually happened.

106
00:07:38,063 --> 00:07:42,427
Sometimes a question doesn't
give a calibration factor.

107
00:07:42,467 --> 00:07:46,752
Instead it tells you to treat
the solution like water:

108
00:07:46,792 --> 00:07:50,997
a density of one point zero
zero gram per millilitre,

109
00:07:51,037 --> 00:07:55,241
and a specific heat capacity
of four point one eight.

110
00:07:55,281 --> 00:08:01,128
Then the heat is m c delta T, using
the total mass of the mixed solution.

111
00:08:01,463 --> 00:08:07,286
For the Q nineteen mixture, the total volume
is seventy-five point zero plus fifty

112
00:08:07,326 --> 00:08:12,434
point zero, which is one hundred and
twenty-five point zero millilitres,

113
00:08:12,474 --> 00:08:16,724
so the mass is one hundred and
twenty-five point zero grams.

114
00:08:16,764 --> 00:08:22,443
q equals one hundred and twenty-five point
zero times four point one eight times

115
00:08:22,483 --> 00:08:27,949
four point three zero: about two thousand
two hundred and forty-seven joules.

116
00:08:27,989 --> 00:08:33,669
Dividing by zero point zero four five zero
moles of water gives delta H of about

117
00:08:33,709 --> 00:08:37,355
negative forty-nine point
nine kilojoules per mole.

118
00:08:37,729 --> 00:08:43,716
Why is that smaller in size than the
negative fifty-six point four we found with

119
00:08:43,756 --> 00:08:45,449
the calibration factor?

120
00:08:45,489 --> 00:08:48,990
The m c delta T model
counts only the solution.

121
00:08:49,030 --> 00:08:53,962
The calibration factor, five hundred
and ninety joules per degree,

122
00:08:54,002 --> 00:08:58,105
also includes the cup, the
thermometer and the stirrer,

123
00:08:58,145 --> 00:09:01,797
which is why it's larger
than the solution alone,

124
00:09:01,837 --> 00:09:04,961
at five hundred and twenty-two point five.

125
00:09:05,001 --> 00:09:09,898
Use the model the question gives
you, and say which one you used.

126
00:09:10,862 --> 00:09:13,844
Now an equation with a coefficient of two.

127
00:09:13,884 --> 00:09:19,314
Two sodium hydroxide plus sulfuric acid
forms sodium sulfate plus two water,

128
00:09:19,354 --> 00:09:25,287
with delta H equal to negative one hundred
and fourteen kilojoules for the equation

129
00:09:25,327 --> 00:09:26,127
as written.

130
00:09:26,462 --> 00:09:29,830
"As written" means one
mole of this reaction:

131
00:09:29,870 --> 00:09:35,131
two moles of sodium hydroxide reacting
with one mole of sulfuric acid,

132
00:09:35,171 --> 00:09:37,176
forming two moles of water.

133
00:09:37,216 --> 00:09:42,780
So one hundred and fourteen kilojoules
is released per two moles of sodium

134
00:09:42,820 --> 00:09:45,622
hydroxide, or per two moles of water.

135
00:09:45,995 --> 00:09:50,652
The amount of reaction that actually
happens is the reaction extent:

136
00:09:50,692 --> 00:09:54,243
the smallest value of amount
divided by coefficient.

137
00:09:54,283 --> 00:10:00,044
Multiply the extent by one hundred and
fourteen kilojoules to get the heat released.

138
00:10:00,084 --> 00:10:05,432
Multiplying the moles of sodium hydroxide
directly by one hundred and fourteen

139
00:10:05,472 --> 00:10:06,991
would double count it.

140
00:10:07,927 --> 00:10:11,571
Practice question Q twenty
is worth six marks.

141
00:10:11,611 --> 00:10:14,494
Pause the video and work through it.

142
00:10:29,927 --> 00:10:32,809
Compare amount divided by coefficient.

143
00:10:32,849 --> 00:10:39,036
Sodium hydroxide: zero point zero three
zero zero over two is zero point zero one

144
00:10:39,076 --> 00:10:39,805
five zero.

145
00:10:39,845 --> 00:10:46,032
Sulfuric acid: zero point zero two zero
zero over one is zero point zero two zero

146
00:10:46,072 --> 00:10:46,416
zero.

147
00:10:46,456 --> 00:10:50,952
Sodium hydroxide gives the
smaller value, so it's limiting,

148
00:10:50,992 --> 00:10:55,835
and the reaction extent is zero
point zero one five zero moles.

149
00:10:56,194 --> 00:11:01,994
Heat released: zero point zero one five
zero moles of reaction times one hundred

150
00:11:02,034 --> 00:11:07,250
and fourteen kilojoules per mole of
reaction is one point seven one zero

151
00:11:07,290 --> 00:11:11,451
kilojoules, or one thousand
seven hundred and ten joules.

152
00:11:11,794 --> 00:11:17,310
The calorimeter gains that heat, so the
temperature rise is q divided by the

153
00:11:17,350 --> 00:11:22,939
calibration factor: one thousand seven
hundred and ten joules divided by four

154
00:11:22,979 --> 00:11:25,644
hundred and eighty joules per degree,

155
00:11:25,684 --> 00:11:29,120
which is three point five
six two five degrees.

156
00:11:29,461 --> 00:11:33,562
The question asks for the final
temperature, not the rise.

157
00:11:33,602 --> 00:11:39,488
Final equals initial plus rise: twenty-one
point zero plus three point five six two

158
00:11:39,528 --> 00:11:45,311
five is twenty-four point five six two
five, about twenty-four point six degrees.

159
00:11:45,661 --> 00:11:52,193
And the acid remaining: zero point zero
two zero zero minus the zero point zero one

160
00:11:52,233 --> 00:11:58,527
five zero moles that reacted leaves zero
point zero zero five zero zero moles of

161
00:11:58,567 --> 00:11:59,676
sulfuric acid.

162
00:12:00,061 --> 00:12:05,248
One mark each for the limiting decision,
the extent, the heat, the rise,

163
00:12:05,288 --> 00:12:08,515
the final temperature
and the acid remaining.

164
00:12:08,555 --> 00:12:14,541
Two traps: multiplying zero point zero
three zero zero moles of sodium hydroxide by

165
00:12:14,581 --> 00:12:18,752
one hundred and fourteen, which
gives twice the true heat,

166
00:12:18,792 --> 00:12:24,601
and reporting the rise of three point five
six degrees as the final temperature.

167
00:12:25,594 --> 00:12:31,501
Suppose we halve the equation: one sodium
hydroxide plus half a sulfuric acid forms

168
00:12:31,541 --> 00:12:34,153
half a sodium sulfate plus one water.

169
00:12:34,193 --> 00:12:38,134
Delta H halves too, to negative
fifty-seven kilojoules.

170
00:12:38,527 --> 00:12:44,288
For the same mixture, the extent of
this halved equation is twice as large:

171
00:12:44,328 --> 00:12:47,227
zero point zero three zero zero moles.

172
00:12:47,267 --> 00:12:53,183
Zero point zero three zero zero times
fifty-seven is one point seven one zero

173
00:12:53,223 --> 00:12:56,626
kilojoules: exactly the
same heat as before.

174
00:12:56,994 --> 00:12:58,495
That's the checkpoint.

175
00:12:58,535 --> 00:13:03,751
Changing the coefficients changes the
delta H associated with the equation,

176
00:13:03,791 --> 00:13:09,287
but it can't change the heat released by
the same physical reaction in the same

177
00:13:09,327 --> 00:13:10,128
calorimeter.

178
00:13:10,168 --> 00:13:13,672
The physical amounts that
react are what they are.

179
00:13:14,660 --> 00:13:17,350
The chain for reaction calorimetry.

180
00:13:17,390 --> 00:13:20,703
Find each amount with
c times V, in litres.

181
00:13:20,743 --> 00:13:24,602
Find the limiting reagent
and the reaction extent.

182
00:13:24,642 --> 00:13:30,139
Measure q for the calorimeter as the
calibration factor times the rise.

183
00:13:30,179 --> 00:13:33,336
The reaction's q is the negative of that.

184
00:13:33,376 --> 00:13:38,913
Then divide by the amount the enthalpy
refers to, and say what that is.

185
00:13:39,260 --> 00:13:40,407
Closing recall.

186
00:13:40,447 --> 00:13:45,390
A reaction forms zero point zero
four zero zero moles of water,

187
00:13:45,430 --> 00:13:49,898
and the calorimeter gains two
point four zero kilojoules.

188
00:13:49,938 --> 00:13:52,628
What is delta H per mole of water?

189
00:14:03,060 --> 00:14:08,579
Negative two point four zero kilojoules
divided by zero point zero four zero zero

190
00:14:08,619 --> 00:14:12,972
moles is negative sixty point zero
kilojoules per mole of water.

191
00:14:13,012 --> 00:14:15,786
Next episode: reading temperature graphs,

192
00:14:15,826 --> 00:14:19,738
and correcting for heat that
escapes during the reaction.
